<div class="contract">

    <div class="contract-section">
        <h2>Training Contract</h2>
        <div class="contract-copy">

            <p>In consideration of my undergoing and receiving a training from THE INSTITUTE FOR ARTS IN THERAPY AND EDUCATION, Sugar House Island, 1 Danes Yard, Stratford, London E15 2QL, I hereby agree as follows:</p>
            
            <h3>Requirements and Conduct</h3>
            <p>1. To meet the requirements for training, personal psychotherapy (must be live, not online) and supervision as laid down for this course.</p>
            <p>2. To adhere to the Code of Relational Interactions, Code of Ethics, Code of Professional Conduct and Code of Professional Practice of IATE (see Student Handbook and overleaf).</p>
            <p>3. Not to betray confidentiality in respect of any information I may receive from, or about, any other trainees at IATE.</p>
            <p>4. To adhere to all the regulations outlined in the General Use and Care of the Premises document in the Student Handbook.</p>
            <p>5. To accept that signing this contract makes me a Member of IATE and is separate from any other training contract with other training or accrediting bodies.</p>
            
            <h3>Personal Process and Engagement</h3>
            <p>6. To accept that at various times during the training, inclusion of my own personal process is integral to the experiential learning provided. This includes personal work in dyads and triads and role-plays in which I will explore my psychological process through imagery and interactions with others.</p>
            <p>7. To actively engage in the experiential sessions. Sitting out or observer state is not permitted.</p>
            <p>8. To ensure that I have sufficient support mechanisms (“go-to” people) in my life to help me with the psychological impact of the training and work-based learning.</p>
            
            <h3>Online Communications</h3>
            <p>9. Due to the safeguarding of fellow students, to ensure that any online chat group (e.g., WhatsApp) is used solely to convey facts about the training and never to communicate about personal material or interpersonal material.</p>
            
            <h3>Policies and Financial Obligations</h3>
            <p>10. To adhere to all of IATE's policies including those relating to payment and cancellation of fees.</p>
            <p>11. To acknowledge that trainees in effect buy a place on the course, which is then not available AT ANY TIME DURING THE COURSE, to any other applicant or anyone on the waiting list. Any trainee therefore, who, for whatever reason, leaves the course, during any of the three academic terms is liable for the balance of the outstanding course fees. If IATE needs to incur costs in retrieving unpaid fees the student will be liable for these costs.</p>
            <p>12. To acknowledge that course fees and fees for related services are reviewed periodically and may be increased, normally at the beginning of an academic year. IATE will provide reasonable advance notice of any increase. Where a course extends over more than one academic year, the fees payable in subsequent academic years may therefore differ from those applicable at the commencement of the course.</p>
            <p>13. To inform IATE immediately if unable to meet the fee obligations or payment deadlines.</p>
            
            <h3>Fee Deferral and Responsibilities</h3>
            <p>14. If, in exceptional circumstances and with IATE’s written agreement, a trainee defers their place to a subsequent academic year, the course fees and any related service fees applicable in the academic year in which they resume their training will apply. Any alternative fee payment arrangements must be authorised in writing by IATE’s Finance Team.</p>
            <p>15. I understand that it is my responsibility to both read and adhere to all policies and regulations laid out in the Student Handbook.</p>
            
            <h3>Therapy and Breach of Contract</h3>
            <p>16. I understand that if I am on a training that requires regular personal therapy and I am not in regular personal therapy for the duration of the course, I am in breach of this contract, which means I run the risk of suspension or dismissal from the course.</p>
            <p>17. I understand that on the extremely rare occasion when IATE feel that it is not appropriate for my training to continue, IATE reserves the right to ask me to leave the training.</p>
            
            <h3>Intellectual Property and Privacy Policy</h3>
            <p>18. By signing this contract, I acknowledge that the copyright and all other intellectual property rights with all course materials are for the sole use of IATE. It is not permitted to take photographs of any of the slide images or make audio recordings of any of the lectures or group discussions. It is not permitted to copy/photocopy course materials, nor use in any other training environment. If any student is found to be doing so it will be treated as a disciplinary issue.</p>
            <p>19. I have read and understand IATE's Privacy Policy as detailed on the IATE website.</p>
            
            <h3>Loss or Personal Injury on the Course</h3>
            <p>IATE regret that we cannot be held responsible for any loss or damage incurred on the course. We cannot be held responsible for any personal injury from personal negligence.</p>
            
            <h3>Postponement of Training Days</h3>
            <p>IATE reserves the right to postpone training days or, where there are insufficient numbers, to cancel the training.</p>
            
            <h3>Changes to Courses</h3>
            <p>IATE will make reasonable efforts to deliver your course as published in the course specification. However, all IATE courses are subject to regular review to ensure that they meet professional regulatory body requirements and remain current and relevant. It may be necessary to make changes in terms of course content and delivery, or staffing changes to accommodate any changes in the curriculum. These changes may be minor as e.g., a change in the timetabling, the location of the teaching rooms, or changes to an assessment because of student or external examiner's feedback. Major changes may include:</p>
            <ul>
                <li>The addition/removal of a core module</li>
                <li>A change to the course or award title</li>
                <li>A change to the credit value of a module/course</li>
                <li>Discontinuing a course</li>
            </ul>
            <p>In all circumstances, we will endeavour to keep any disruption to a minimum, to inform you of any changes at the earliest opportunity and to inform you what has changed and why.</p>
            
            <p>Thank you for your understanding and we very much hope you enjoy the training!</p>
            
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Code of Conduct -->

    <div class="contract-section">
        <h2>Code of Relational Transactions and Personal Readiness Criteria to Qualify from Your Training</h2>
        <div class="contract-copy">
            
            <h3>Interacting with Staff and Peers on the Training: Why It Matters</h3>
            <p>It matters because being a member of a helping profession requires the highest level of relational skills. We need to be sure that trainees can transact at all times in socially engaged, not socially defended ways and especially when under stress. This is because there will inevitably be situations of stress in the workplace and sometimes in transactions with clients. We want trainees to transact with others in such a way that they add to the mental health of any culture or community, and never mar that culture.</p>
            <p>Furthermore, there will be times of course, when a trainee will need to put a boundary down with and/or gracefully confront a client. We are truly a listening and talking cure profession so it's vital we do this in a socially engaging not socially defensive ways, thinking carefully about the tone and language we use.</p>
            
            <h3>Personal Readiness Criteria</h3>
            <p>1. To have a warm, calm, embodied therapeutic presence.</p>
            <p>2. To show clear evidence of having mastered the art of active listening and being able to make empathic responses, holding in mind the mind of the other.</p>
            
            <h3>Frequently Asked Questions</h3>
            <h4>Can I get angry on the training?</h4>
            <p>Of course, you can get angry. It simply means you get angry from an adult ego state rather than from critical parent ego state (see TA Today Chapter 2 and 3*). So you would say something like, “When you …I feel xx… so what I want is… will you?” i.e., the intention to resolve, rather than communication with the underlying message of “You are xx and I will MAKE you change and if you don't I will get angry” i.e., the intention to punish and make the other person feel bad, shamed, guilty, controlled.</p>
            <p>If you do get triggered and transact with archaic anger not healthy here and now anger you would be able to stand back and reflect in order to have insight into how your past trauma has coloured your perception of the present moment. From there, you would move into repair and resolution.</p>
            
            <h4>Can we criticize or give feedback?</h4>
            <p>We love feedback about our courses and have asked for feedback from the start of IATE 30 years ago, year of conception: 1989. Over the years, we have changed content, structure and staffing on our courses as a result of trainee feedback. Some courses have now benefitted from 30 years of trainee feedback! In contrast, when feedback is delivered from a critical, superior, blaming, shaming, controlling way, we consider this to be a personal readiness concern for all the reasons stated above.</p>
            
            <h4>Are we expected to be best friends?</h4>
            <p>Absolutely not. You may really not like a peer or member of staff. It's human nature. It's then how you handle this with grace and respect.</p>
            
            <p>Thank you for reading this. We are sure you will understand the importance of the above. Unlike other trainings outside of the helping professions, psychotherapy and counselling require in-depth self-knowledge, self-reflection and transactional expertise at the highest level.</p>
            
            <p style="font-style:italic;">*Transactional Analysis is taught as part of many training programmes at IATE. You. TA Today by Ian Steward and Vann Joines is a key text. You will find the key chapters on Moodle. Please read Part 1, 2, 3 and chapter 18</p>
            
        </div>
    </div>
